  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts. It typechecks, but I couldn't build it or run it in a browser, so none of the rendering, colours or lighting has been seen.\n\n- **Islands:** There are 9 islands of different sizes. Each has a grass disc, a rock rim, a squashed rock lump and an inverted cone underside, plus two hanging rock cones. They carry simple trees (cone canopies on trunks) and emissive crystals. Each island bobs on its own speed, amplitude and phase, with a slight tilt.\n- **Sky and sun:** A gradient sky runs from orange at the horizon to purple overhead, with a pink band. The sun is a large custom-shader sphere with an additive glow sphere, and neither is affected by fog. Fill and sun-direction lights are placed to suit the sunset.\n- **Haze:** Exponential lavender-blue fog makes far islands paler and bluer.\n- **Clouds:** There are 12 cloud banks, each a group of squashed translucent ellipsoids. They sit at depths from 8 to 230 units from the origin and drift sideways at different speeds, wrapping around at the edges.\n- **Waterfall:** The largest island has a thin custom-shader ribbon with scrolling streaks, plus 40 small falling droplets (one node each, updated every frame).\n- **Camera:** The camera is a fixed perspective camera. Every island and cloud swings around a vertical axis at z = -70, with the angle following a sine wave of ±0.32 rad (about 18°) over a 50-second loop. This looks like the camera moving in an arc. I did it this way because `llms.txt` says not to use `app.camera`, and `camera.orbit` with `autoRotate` would swing the camera 360° and turn the sun out of view.\n- **Ready flag and performance:** `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after `await app.ready()`. The scene has no shadows and roughly 400 simple nodes.\n\nTwo things most likely to look off when first run are the lighting and fog balance (I tuned them blind) and the sun and waterfall shaders. Custom GLSL on primitive spheres and boxes is assumed to work as it does on the documented custom geometry.",
